cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use Ghost Lows, please cite the software (this file). Benchmark results should
  also name the benchmark version they were computed on. Cite the datasets and
  simulator you use as listed in docs/reference/DATA.md and docs/CITATIONS.md.
  Co-authors: Pranav Reddy and Cohen Ferrari.
title: "Ghost Lows: an open benchmark for real-time CGM low-glucose alarms under sensor faults"
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Pranav
    family-names: Reddy
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0007-5767-5438"
    affiliation: "University of California, Santa Barbara"
  - given-names: Cohen
    family-names: Ferrari
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-8060-9907"
    affiliation: "University of California, Santa Barbara"
version: 0.3.0
date-released: 2026-10-27
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23573707
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/pkarakala/ghost-lows"
abstract: >-
  Continuous glucose monitors (CGMs) can report false lows, often when the wearer lies on
  the sensor (pressure-induced sensor attenuation, PISA, or compression lows), and real-time
  low-glucose alarms then fire without a true low. Ghost Lows is an open benchmark that
  scores such alarms on lows caught, false alarms per day and minutes of warning under a
  mixed sensor-fault suite (compression, dropout, stale readings, drift, spikes), with a
  controlled ablation of what insulin and carbohydrate context adds. In 30 virtual patients
  (UVA/Padova simulator) with a compression low every night, compression caused 0.32 (95% CI
  0.30–0.35) of a trend alarm's 1.53 (1.33–1.73) false alarms per day; insulin and
  carbohydrate context never cut compression false alarms by the pre-registered 25%, and
  published compression detectors reduced them only by missing severe lows (post hoc).
  Includes the benchmark (ghostlows-bench v1), its results, checks on two public CGM
  datasets, a Python package and an upload report that runs in the browser. Research
  software; not a medical device.
keywords:
  - continuous glucose monitoring
  - CGM
  - hypoglycemia
  - hypoglycaemia
  - low-glucose alarms
  - false alarms
  - pressure-induced sensor attenuation
  - PISA
  - compression artifacts
  - compression artefacts
  - sensor faults
  - type 1 diabetes
  - "UVA/Padova simulator"
  - benchmark
  - Python
